 "observations": [
  "The page number «163» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 52», in bold sans serif, underlined. At the foot, the signature «21*» on the right; recorded here, not transcribed as a line. The titles (L01-L04), in bold, the sigla in roman: N's title, «Tadil (C)» and «Examinatio Mercurii (C)»; L04, «Locus1) draconis eius 0. 21. 10», in smaller bold type, followed by «(CON)». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of L03 and L04, «C 68v, O 126r,» and «N 34r.», the letters raised; here as right margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 162 (JP2 0194), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Semita numerorum (C)»; «Sublimatio ascensus2) Mercurii examinata3)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza4)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Mercurii»; «Latitudo Mercurii ascendentis5)» over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L05-L09), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L10, the units, in bold.",
  "L11-L40. Checked as on p. 162 (JP2 0194): the Sublimatio is 7s 14° 54′ less half the Examinatio argumenti in every row, a half minute counted as a whole, except at 41° (L21), where 7s 9° 34′ stands for 7s 9° 34½′, with the edition's 10° 39′ (note d, C, against O's 32); C's 7s 9° 32′ (note b) is 2½′ less. The Examinatio argumenti agrees with the equation of the epicycle of radius 21.97 of p. 162 to within 1.1′ in every row; the radius that reproduces each value is between 21.94 and 22.01. The Latitudo secunda is 6° 15′ · sin x to within 0.5″ in every row.",
  "L11-L40, the other columns: the Examinatio centri increases by 2′ to 4′ a degree to 3° 27′ at 60°; it lies between 0.4′ below and 2.9′ above 3° 56′ · sin x. The Statio prima decreases from 4s 26° 34′ to 4s 25° 18′ by 2′ to 4′ a degree. The Latitudo prima is 1° 41′ to 33°, 1° 40′ to 38°, 1° 39′ to 41°, 1° 38′ to 45°, 1° 37′ to 49°, 1° 36′ to 52°, 1° 35′ to 55°, 1° 34′ to 58° and 1° 33′ at 59° and 60°.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 2s 0°, is N's; C and O have 1. Checked: O's 7s 9° 27′ at 42° (note c, against C's 37) agrees with the rule; at 49° (note e) both O's 12° 34′ and C's 35 agree with the Sublimatio of the row, 7s 8° 37′, and the steps of 14′ and the epicycle, 12° 33.7′, favour the 34 of the edition; C's 15° 1′ at 60° (note f, against O's 50) agrees with the Sublimatio, 7s 7° 24′, and with the epicycle, 15° 0.3′. The Statio prima 4s 26° 17′ at 38° and 4s 25° 18′ at 60° (notes g and h, O and N) fit the steps of the column; the Latitudo secunda 4° 25′ 10″ at 45° (note i, O and N, against C's 35), 4° 25′ 9.9″ computed, and 5° 10′ 53″ at 56° (note k), the edition's 53 against the 23 of C and O, 5° 10′ 53.4″ computed; the note does not say «correxi».",
  "Italics: «om.». L02, «Tadil (C)»; L10, «Gr» in the units of the Diminutio and «Sig» in the units of the Statio prima, without their points; N04, «ON. 35 C»: see the critical notes."
 ],
